    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5462\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to exemptions from the\nreduction of school aid\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nFor the purposes of state aid eligibility, this legislation authorizes\nthe Commissioner of Education to excuse up to five days from the 180\nday requirement for school closures related to the H1N1 (swine flu)\noutbreak that required the school's closure to protect the health,\nsafety and well-being of its staff and students during the 2008-09\nschool year.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the Education Law to give the\nCommissioner of Education the authority to excuse up to 5 missed days\nas a result of a closure due to the H1N1 (swine flu) during the\n2008-09 school year.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nState law requires 180 days of instruction to maintain eligibility for\nthe full amount of their state aid entitlement. The financial\napportionment from the state is reduced by 1/180th for each day less\nthan 180 days the school actually is in session. This legislation\ngives the Commissioner of Education the authority to disregard this\nreduction for up to five days in the event a school is unable to make\nup the lost days of instruction due to its decision to close the\nschool to protect the health and safety of staff and student during\nschool closures when swine flu has been identified in the 2008-09\nschool year. Because of the timing of this outbreak, many districts\nhave no unexpended \"snow days\" and have already engaged in spring\nbreak, and therefore have very little flexibility to make up these\ndays.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
